        cuisine='World-renowned Syrian gastronomy (Halabi cuisine of Aleppo is regarded as one of the culinary capitals of the world): Kibbeh (over twenty varieties: Kibbeh Safarjaliyya with quince and lamb, Kibbeh Mabrouma with pistachios, baked, fried, or raw / Nayyeh), Shawarma (shaved spiced meat roasted on a vertical spit, served in thin flatbread with garlic toum sauce), Fatteh (layered toasted pita, boiled chickpeas, and tangy garlic yogurt sauce topped with sizzling pine nuts and clarified butter), Yabrak (delicate vine leaves stuffed with spiced minced lamb and rice, simmered with garlic cloves and lemon), Mahshi (hollowed courgettes and eggplants stuffed with meat and rice), Fattoush, Hummus with spiced minced meat and pine nuts, Mutabal, and world-famous sweets like Baklava, Knafeh, and Booza (stretchy mastic-and-salep ice cream rolled in crushed emerald pistachios, legendary at Bakdash in Al-Hamidiyah Souq).',
        festivals=['Evacuation Day / National Day (April 17)', 'Damascus International Fair (historic international trade exhibition founded in 1954)', 'Silk Road Festival', 'Cotton Festival in Aleppo', 'Saidnaya Christian pilgrimage festivals', 'Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha'],
        music='Classical Arabic Tarab and Muwashahat (poetic musical form perfected in Aleppo); legendary vocal master Sabah Fakhri (guinness-record holding maestro of classical Arab song); Farid al-Atrash and Asmahan (historic icons); classical Oud and Qanun virtuosos.',
        dance='Dabke (passionate, energetic national folk line dance performed with shoulder shakes and synchronized stomps to the beat of the Mijwiz reed pipe and Derbake drum at every celebration); traditional sword-and-shield dance (Ardah Halabiya / Raqs al-Sayf wa al-Turs).',
        art='Damascus mosaic woodwork (intricate marquetry furniture inlaid with mother-of-pearl and contrasting woods); Damascene steel sword blades (legendary ancient metallurgy); brass and copper repoussé; modern Syrian fine art movement (Louay Kayyali, Fateh Moudarres, Marwan Kassab-Bachi).',
        architecture='Umayyad Mosque of Damascus (one of the largest and oldest mosques in the world, with dazzling golden glass mosaics depicting paradise, four soaring minarets, and the shrine of John the Baptist / Prophet Yahya); Citadel of Aleppo (magnificent medieval fortress rising on a 50m mound above the city); Krak des Chevaliers (world\'s best-preserved medieval Crusader castle, UNESCO); traditional Damascene courtyard houses (Bayt Dimashqi) with central fountains, jasmine vines, and alternating black and white stone (Ablaq).',
        literature='Ancient Ugaritic epic poetry; golden age of classical Arabic poetry (Al-Mutanabbi, Abu Tammam, Al-Ma\'arri); modern romantic and political master Nizar Qabbani; acclaimed playwright Saadallah Wannous; contemporary poet Adonis (Ali Ahmad Said Esber); novelist Zakaria Tamer.',
        cinema='The Syrian National Film Organization produced critically acclaimed cinema masterpieces: directors Mohammad Malas ("The Night", "Dreams of the City"), Abdellatif Abdelhamid, and Omar Amiralay; renowned television drama industry (Bab Al-Hara - the most watched television series in Arab history).',
        sports='Football (passionate national following: Syrian national team "Qasioun Eagles" reached the AFC Asian Cup knockout stages in 2024; Al-Karamah and Al-Ittihad clubs); Weightlifting (Man Asaad won Olympic Bronze at Tokyo 2020 in the +109kg class); Track and Field (Ghada Shouaa won Syria\'s first-ever Olympic Gold medal in the heptathlon in Atlanta 1996); Wrestling; Boxing; Basketball.',
        media='SANA (Syrian Arab News Agency), Syrian Television, Alikhbaria Syria, Al-Watan newspaper, Al-Thawra, and Tishreen.',
        clothing='Traditional rural attire: Sherwal (baggy trousers) with embroidered waistcoat and Keffiyeh headscarf for men; colorful embroidered Thob for women; in cities, modern tailored suits and European fashion alongside modest conservative Abayas and Hijabs.',
        holidays=['Evacuation Day (April 17)', 'Revolution Day (March 8)', 'Martyrs\' Day (May 6)', 'October Liberation War (October 6)', 'Christmas Day', 'Easter (both Catholic and Orthodox)', 'Eid al-Fitr', 'Eid al-Adha', 'New Year\'s Day'],
        crafts='Aleppo laurel soap (Ghar soap - world\'s oldest handmade hard soap, made from olive oil and laurel oil since antiquity), Damascene mother-of-pearl inlaid woodwork, Damascus brocade silk weaving (famous for Queen Elizabeth II\'s wedding dress fabric), and blown glass.',
        worldHeritageSites=['Ancient City of Damascus', 'Ancient City of Aleppo', 'Site of Palmyra (ancient oasis ruins, colonnaded street, Temple of Bel)', 'Ancient City of Bosra (Roman black basalt theatre)', 'Crac des Chevaliers and Qal’at Salah El-Din', 'Ancient Villages of Northern Syria (The Dead Cities)'],
        unescoIntangible=['Practices and craftsmanship associated with the Damascene rose in Mrah', 'Traditional Syrian glassblowing (inscribed 2023)'],
        famousPeople=['Nizar Qabbani', 'Queen Zenobia', 'Sabah Fakhri', 'Saladin', 'Adonis (poet)', 'Ghada Shouaa (Olympic champion)', 'Bashar al-Assad', 'Farid al-Atrash', 'Asmahan'],
        flagDescription='A horizontal tricolor consisting of three equal bands of red (top, symbolizing the sacrifices of the nation), white (middle, peace and purity), and black (bottom, the dark past of foreign oppression), charged in the white band with two green five-pointed stars centered horizontally (originally symbolizing the historic union between Syria and Egypt in the United Arab Republic).',
        coatOfArmsDescription='The golden Hawk of Quraish facing sinister, bearing on its breast a heraldic shield with the national flag depicted vertically with two green stars, clutching a green scroll in its talons inscribed "Syrian Arab Republic" in Arabic calligraphy.',
        symbols={'animal': 'Syrian brown bear and Arabian horse', 'bird': 'Northern bald ibis', 'tree': 'Olive tree (Olea europaea) and Aleppo pine', 'flower': 'Damask rose (Ward Jori / Rosa damascena - world\'s most fragrant rose, source of pure rose oil)'},
        landmarks=['Umayyad Mosque of Damascus (The Great Mosque of Damascus)', 'Citadel of Aleppo (monumental medieval fortress)', 'Krak des Chevaliers (Crusader fortress, Homs)', 'Ancient Ruins of Palmyra (Tadmur)', 'Roman Theatre of Bosra (carved from black basalt)', 'Al-Hamidiyah Souq and Bakdash ice cream (Damascus)', 'Ma\'loula cliff-hanging monastery (where Aramaic is spoken)', 'Norias of Hama (giant ancient wooden waterwheels on the Orontes)'],
        travelNotes='Travel security advisories recommend extreme caution due to residual armed conflict zones and geopolitical instability; consular visas are required in advance; Damascus, Latakia, Tartus, and central Aleppo maintain operational civilian hotels and restaurants; US dollar cash is widely used for major expenses, but exchange must adhere to local banking regulations; independent travel is recommended only with vetted local guides and registration.',
        shortSummary='Syria is an immortal cradle of human civilization nestled in the Fertile Crescent, famed for the five-thousand-year heritage of Damascus and Aleppo, the ancient monumental stone arches of Palmyra, exquisite Damascene brocade and laurel soap, and the enduring resilience of its diverse, warm-hearted people.'
